  available under MPL-2.0. Our own files that merely use it are unaffected.

  We do not modify bitECS. If that ever changes, the modified files must be
  published under MPL-2.0 and this notice updated to say where.

Engine Simulator — https://github.com/ange-yaghi/engine-sim — MIT
Copyright 2022 AngeTheGreat (Ange Yaghi)

  The combustion engine you hear in corridor, compiled to WebAssembly and
  shipped as packages/enginesim/wasm/enginesim.wasm. With it, under the same
  MIT licence and the same copyright: piranha (the scripting language),
  simple-2d-constraint-solver (the rigid-body solver), and the engine
  definitions and impulse-response library embedded in that binary.

  We build against Open Engine Simulator (https://github.com/zabayone/open-engine-sim),
  the community fork, which is MIT and preserves the upstream notice. We do not
  modify any of it: the build fetches the pinned sources and links against them.
  Our own code in packages/enginesim — the C API, the audio worklet, the
  gearbox, the bench — is Apache-2.0.

  MIT requires the copyright notice and permission text to travel with the
  software. They do: packages/enginesim/wasm/LICENSE-engine-sim.txt ships beside
  the binary and is served with it, and CREDITS.md says what is inside.

Procedural Water — https://github.com/tuxalin/water-shader — MIT
Copyright (c) 2017 tuxalin

  The Gerstner wave normal, the wavelength-dependent colour extinction and the
  shore-foam approach in corridor's water (apps/corridor/src/world/waterShader.ts,
  wired into water.ts) are ported from this water shader. No file, texture or
  shader source from it is used verbatim: the waves are evaluated analytically
  here and driven by a per-vertex (depth, flow direction) attribute corridor
  bakes at build time. The MIT notice travels in that file's header and is
  restated in CREDITS.md.

